    "denominator_EJR": "Essentially resolved at s(N, W_2) = 58. Almost all colleagues have converged or acknowledged that 4-voter subsets being 8-cohesive forces at least 5 voters to have utility >= 8, which with c = 6 requires 10 private candidates: 5*8 + 3*6 = 58.",
    "numerator_Core": "Major split between 65 (Colleagues 1, 3, 4, 8, 10, 12, 15, 18, 19, 20) and 72 / 36/29 (Colleagues 2, 5, 7, 9, 13, 14, 16, 17, 21)."
  },
  "core_dispute_analysis": {
    "camp_72_argument": "Advocates for 72 argue that the core requires weak Pareto blocking (a coalition blocks if every member is weakly better off and at least one strictly better off), or that coalition N with budget 16 can guarantee (9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9), which weakly dominates (9,8,8,8,8,8,8,8). They argue that leaving 7 members at 8 is unstable or violates Pareto efficiency.",
    "camp_65_argument": "Advocates for 65 rely on the standard definition of the Core in approval-based committee voting (Aziz et al. 2017, Peters 2018): a coalition S blocks W if there exists T with |T| <= (k/n)|S| such that |A(i) \cap T| > |A(i) \cap W| strictly for ALL i \in S.",
    "mathematical_verification_of_65": [
      "Coalition N (size 8, budget 16): to strictly improve voter 1 (utility 9), T must give voter 1 utility 10. Since voter 1 approves only 10 candidates total (8 common + 2 private), T MUST contain all 10 candidates approved by voter 1.",
      "This leaves exactly 16 - 10 = 6 spots in T.",
      "To strictly improve voters 2..8 (each currently having utility 8), each needs at least 9 approvals. The 8 common candidates provide 8, so each needs at least 1 private candidate.",
      "Since voters 2..8 have mutually disjoint private candidates, giving 9 to all 7 voters requires 7 private candidates.",
      "However, only 6 spots remain in T. Thus, at most 6 of the 7 voters can reach 9; at least one voter remains at 8 and does not strictly improve.",
      "Therefore, coalition N CANNOT strictly improve all its members!",
      "Subcoalition S not containing voter 1: |S| <= 7, members need >= 9 approvals, costing at least 8 + |S|. For 8 + |S| <= 2|S|, we need |S| >= 8, impossible for |S| <= 7.",
      "Subcoalition S containing voter 1: members need >= 9, voter 1 needs 10, costing 10 + (|S| - 1) = |S| + 9 <= 2|S| => |S| >= 9, impossible.",
      "Conclusion: Under the standard axiomatic definition of the Core in social choice (strict blocking for all members), the committee with c = 7, p = 9 is fully core-stable."
    ]
  },
  "strategy_for_next_round": {
    "points_to_make": [
      "Reiterate the exact literature definition of the Core (Aziz et al. 2017, Peters 2018): blocking requires STRICT inequality for ALL members of the deviating coalition.",
      "Explicitly present the mathematical impossibility proof that N cannot strictly improve all 8 members with a budget of 16 (voter 1 requires 10 seats, leaving only 6 seats for 7 disjoint private candidates).",
      "Explain the distinction between the Core (strict blocking, weakly Pareto efficient) and the Strong Core (weak blocking / Pareto blocking, which is often empty in multiwinner voting).",
      "Maintain firm stance on 65/58."
    ]
  }
}

