      "Camp 72 also argues that the c = 7, p = 9 committee is weakly Pareto-dominated by replacing 1 private candidate with candidate 8, asserting that the Core must be strongly Pareto optimal."
    ],
    "camp_65_counterproof_and_literature_facts": [
      "In the canonical ABC literature (Aziz et al. 2017; Peters 2018; Lackner & Skowron 2023, Def 4.1), the Core is universally defined by STRICT improvement for ALL members of the blocking coalition S: \\forall i \\in S, |A(i) \\cap T| > |A(i) \\cap W|.",
      "The weak-dominance version is called the 'Strict Core' (or 'Core under weak blocking') in cooperative game theory, which is known to be frequently empty in ABC voting and is explicitly NOT the standard definition.",
      "Under the standard Core definition, the Core implies only WEAK Pareto efficiency (no committee can strictly improve ALL voters in V). To strictly improve all 10 voters would require budget 8 + 2 + 7 + 3 + 3 = 23 > 20, so the c = 7 committee is weakly Pareto optimal and cannot be blocked by the grand coalition.",
      "Mathematical proof of stability for c = 7, p = 9: Grand coalition N needs 10 for voter 1 (cost 10) and >= 9 for voters 2..8 (cost 7), totaling 17 > 16. Any subcoalition S of size s needs at least 8 + s <= 2s => s >= 8 (if omitting voter 1) or s + 9 <= 2s => s >= 9 (if containing voter 1), both impossible."
    ]
  },
  "current_verdict": {
    "core_minimum": 65,
